## How I work

I care about the decisions that are expensive to reverse. Schema design, tenant
isolation, where the queue boundary sits, what happens when a job fails halfway
through. Those choices decide whether a system is still pleasant to work on two
years later, and they are much cheaper to get right at the start than to
retrofit.

That bias shows up in what I have built. At AdsCult I designed a multi-tenant
enterprise CRM from an empty repository through five production modules, and the
thing I am most pleased with is not a feature — it is that tenant isolation is
enforced at the database layer, so a mistake in application code cannot leak one
client's data into another's.

## What I am doing now

Two things, in parallel.

The first is **Vashix**, a media verification platform I am designing and
building end to end. It hardware-signs photos and video at the moment of
capture, sealing who took it, where, when, and that it has not been edited
since — verified against the C2PA 2.1 provenance standard. It is the most
technically demanding thing I have worked on: device attestation, an Android
SDK, and a trust-scoring engine that has to be defensible rather than merely
plausible.

The second is going deeper on AWS. I already run production infrastructure —
EC2, RDS, S3, SQS, Nginx as a reverse proxy — but running it and being able to
reason rigorously about it are different things. I am working through the
Solutions Architect Associate material and writing up the parts that connect to
systems I have actually operated, which is where most of the [engineering
writing](/engineering) on this site comes from.

## AI, specifically

I have shipped RAG in production rather than in a notebook: Pinecone and
pgvector for semantic retrieval, LLM APIs wired into real workflows, and the
unglamorous work around them — chunking that respects document structure,
evaluating retrieval quality, and deciding when a vector search is genuinely
better than a well-written SQL query. Often it is not, and saying so has saved
more time than any model choice.

I also use AI heavily in how I build. Claude Code is part of my daily workflow,
and I hold a certification from Anthropic's education programme in it.

## Before this

I started with a BSc in Information Technology from KES Shroff College,
finishing with a 9.0 CGPA and a Best Project Award. Two internships before my
current role — MERN stack work and payment gateway integrations at Prabhavti
Digital Services, and mobile-first frontend work at Webriver Solutions — taught
me the parts of the job that are not architecture: shipping to a deadline,
reading someone else's code without complaining about it, and writing tests for
the flows that actually break.

## Outside the editor

I read a lot of post-mortems. There is more to learn from a well-written
incident report than from most conference talks — they are the only place the
industry writes honestly about what went wrong.

If you are working on something where the architecture matters,
[I would like to hear about it](/contact).
